messages:
maya: Heads up — CI helm render/lint started dropping nodeSelector/affinity when we rely on our "defaults" helper and set those keys to null. Ended up scheduling GPU pods onto cpu nodes in eu-west, which messed up throughput. Repro and snippets below.

maya: Repro (local helm template + pipeline):
```
# templates/_helpers.tpl
{{- define "mychart.defaults" -}}
{{- $defaults := dict "resources" (dict) "nodeSelector" nil "affinity" nil -}}
{{- return (merge $defaults .Values) -}}
{{- end -}}
```

maya: With values.yml that leaves nodeSelector: null the rendered podSpec ends up with no nodeSelector/affinity block at all. In one staging rollout that caused GPUs to go to non-GPU nodes.

chris: which helm version are we linting with? older helm3 had weird merge behaviour with nil vs empty map. :eyes:

maya: CI uses helm v3.9.4 in the pipeline image. local repro with 3.9.4 matches pipeline.

chris: also check values.schema.json — our schema currently allows nodeSelector to be either object or null via anyOf, right? that can confuse validators/transformers.

maya: yup schema snippet: 
```
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "nodeSelector": {
      "anyOf": [
        { "type": "object" },
        { "type": "null" }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

ara: we've hit this before. The problem is twofold: Helm's sprig/merge semantics treat nil differently, and our downstream sanitizer (values-sanitizer step in the pipeline) prunes null keys before rendering, which means merge doesn't see the key at all. pipeline+kustomize then ends up without the map. Recommend normalizing to empty map at the helper boundary.

ara: quick fix in helpers:
```
{{- define "mychart.defaults" -}}
{{- $defaults := dict "resources" (dict) "nodeSelector" (dict) "affinity" (dict) -}}
{{- return (merge $defaults .Values) -}}
{{- end -}}
```

ara: or coalesce on render: `{{ .Values.nodeSelector | default (dict) | toYaml }}` so we never emit `null`.

julian: +1 to avoiding nulls. I'd also change values.schema to not allow null for affinity/nodeSelector — prefer an empty object default. k8s expects object when present. Allowing null just hides intent and breaks downstream tools.

luis: FYI the sanitizer was intentionally pruning nulls to keep manifests small, but that's breaking semantics for charts that use null to signal fallbacks. We should either update the sanitizer to keep keys present if chart declares them in defaults, or change charts to set explicit empty maps. I favour fixing charts where possible; less surprising.

deploy-bot: Pipeline run -> https://ci.redwood.local/runs/71234 (failed helm-lint step) — logs show values sanitiser removing keys: `removed null key: nodeSelector`

maya: Thanks all. I'll open a small PR to change the helper to emit empty maps by default and update the schema to expect objects (not null). I'll also add a CI test to run `helm template` + our sanitizer to catch this in future.

chris: sounds good. If anyone wants to pair on the sanitizer change, ping me. we should also add a doc blurb in chart authoring guides about "avoid null for maps". :memo:

maya: Will do. PR incoming this afternoon. Thanks!
